  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nFollowing the passage of last nights cold front, a \"cooler\" and \ndrier airmass has settled into the area today. A 1008mb surface low \ncentered across Iowa this afternoon has helped steer the breezy \nnortherly winds for today (gusts as high as 30-45MPH). These \ngusty winds will gradually lighten through the rest of the \nafternoon and evening hours. Aloft, a shortwave trough (the \ndisturbance responsible for developing last nights storms) \ncontinues to shift east and away. This will leave space for a \nsouthwest U.S. ridge to grow and expand up into the Central \nPlains area over the weekend. Rising pressure from this building\nridge will help maintain dry conditions as subsidence works to \nkeep the skies mostly clear. In addition, a mini warm up will \ntranspire from Friday to Monday as sunny skies and southerly \nturning surface winds return (a mix of warm air advection and \ndiurnal heating). Highs between today and Monday will gradually \nincrease a few degrees each day, residing mostly in the 90s by \nMonday. A few far southeast Nebraska and western Kansas \nlocations could even near the low triple digits (30- 60% \nchance). \n\nThe next major pattern change will come Tuesday as a northwest U.S. \ntrough is favored to push into the central and northern Plains. The \nnext chance for precipitation in the forecast will come with this \npattern shift Tuesday night as a cold front looks to pass out ahead \nof the upper-level disturbance. Our chances for precipitation for \nTuesday night has increased slightly for our Nebraska areas\n(20%-40% for areas just north of the state line and up to \n50-65% for locations north of I-80). This slight increase in \nconfidence comes as ensemble model guidance begins to narrows \ndown on the timing of the arrival of the aforementioned trough. \nGiven that this event falls outside of the range for higher- \nresolution short-term model guidance, we are not able to \nprovide many additional details in regard to specifics (coverage\nor intensity) at this time. This event, however, will be the \nareas next best potential for scattered storms. \n\nModel confidence past Tuesday is a little more uncertain (widening \ngap between the 25th and 75th NBM percentiles or wider range of \nmodeled outcomes). This increase in uncertainty comes as the the two \nleading LREF clusters begin to show diverging scenarios to how the \nmid-week trough may progress. Depending on how long this \nfeature lingers over the region, its presence will impact the \nforecast through the rest of the week. The leading \ncluster/scenario (weighted more on the ENS & GEPS ensembles) is \nleaning more on a drier and warmer end to next week with the \nsecond leading cluster/scenario (weighted more towards the GEFS)\nleans toward a more mild and wetter end to next week. We will\ncontinue to iron out the details for the mid to end of week \nforecast as the cone of uncertainty begins to narrow more.",
